  "text": "I'll review the round-9 Publish fixes against the ship bar, starting with the synthesis, the spec, and how push destination is decided.The last four rounds each blocked on Publish reading a different destination than it checked. I'll read those findings and the current push path before trying to break the fix.The push path is pinned to `GIT_DIR`, but the restart check may not be. I'll compare those paths and probe repo settings that can still move a literal URL.Relative push URLs resolve against the current directory. Round 9 runs the push inside `.git`, so I\u2019ll check whether Publish can report success for a different repository than the one that URL names from the project.FIX FIRST \u2014 a relative remote URL is resolved from the git directory, so Publish records success for the URL the user confirmed while the branch is created in a different repository.\n\n## BLOCKING FINDINGS\n\n1. **High, PROVEN.** `worktrees.py:1403` (`_repo_git` cwd), used by the push at `1567` and the confirming `ls-remote` at `1577`. `resolve_op` repeats it at `2081` with `cwd=common_dir` and no `GIT_DIR`. Git resolves a relative URL against the process cwd. Round 9 set that cwd to the repository's git dir (`<repo>/.git`). From the checkout, `../proj.git` is the sibling the user added with `git remote add` and that `git push origin` updates. From `<repo>/.git`, the same string is `<repo>/proj.git`, inside the working tree. `rewrite_rule`'s `ls-remote --get-url` (`1441`) compares the text and does not absolutize, and `transport_override` does not look at this, so both return `None`. The dialog shows the relative string (`static/app.js:1519`).\n\n   **Trigger.** Origin's push URL is a relative path, such as `../proj.git`. The agent (or any writer in the checkout) runs `git init --bare` at `<repo>/proj.git`. No transport key changes.\n\n   **What goes wrong.** `wt.push` returns success, the push op is `done`, and `published.url` is `../proj.git`. The sibling bare repo never receives `refs/heads/corral/fix-login`. The decoy inside the checkout has it, at the reviewed oid `736832d8`. With no decoy, Publish errors (`rc 128`, \"`../proj.git` does not appear to be a git repository\") even though that sibling exists and already has `main`. On restart, an `intent` push of that URL is settled as done (\"the remote has it\") when only the decoy has the oid. The isolated suite, 335 tests, passed. `test_BB8_10` locks this cwd in, so it stays green.\n\n   **Minimal fix.** Keep `GIT_DIR` pinned to the common dir. For any push URL git would treat as a local relative path, resolve it against `entry[\"repo_top\"]` (the directory `git push origin` uses), show that absolute path in the dialog, and pass only the absolute path to `git push` and to both `ls-remote` checks. Point `resolve_op` through that same path.\n\n   **Repro.** `scratch/probe_r10_rel_push.py` (Publish success against the decoy). `scratch/probe_r10_resolve.py` (restart settlement). `scratch/probe_r10_rel.py` (cwd matrix: checkout to the sibling, git dir to `<repo>/proj.git`).\n\n## NON-BLOCKING\n\n1. `test_BB6_13` accepts reason `rewrite` or `transport`, so a broken `--null` parser still passes.\n2. A regular config swapped after the checks and restored before the second fingerprint is still invisible (suspected since round 8; not reproduced).\n3. Any repo-local `http.*`, `url.*`, or `include.*` refuses Publish, including a harmless `http.postBuffer` or a shared `include.path`.\n4. Global and system git config, `GIT_SSH_COMMAND`, and `~/.ssh/config` still steer a push (stated boundary).\n5. A `pre-push` hook or `hook.*.command` can copy the commit during Publish (spec D5). The push git itself still uses the URL it was given.\n6. A confirmed http(s) server can redirect the pack (`http.followRedirects` defaults to `initial`). The confirming `ls-remote` follows that redirect too.\n7. `remote.*.proxy`, `remote.*.receivepack`, `remote.*.mirror`, and `remote.*.vcs` do not apply to a literal URL (probed).\n8. Stated choices that still hold: a non-dumpable process is skipped by the Discard scan. Discard may refuse once while files are still changing. A pane whose agent switched branches stays blocked, Discard included, until the branch is switched back.\n\nA checkout whose origin is a relative path such as `../proj.git` will fail Publish against a remote that `git push` from that checkout accepts, or it will mark the branch published when only a repository inside the working tree received it.",
